{"retractions" [[17592186332144 :question/problem "Find the singular solution to the following Clairaut differential equation:\n\n$$\ny=x{y}^{′}+{y}^{′}-3.\n$$"] [17592186332144 :question/worked-solution "A Clairaut differential equation has the form\n\n$$\ny=x{y}^{′}+g({y}^{′}),\n$$\n\nwhere $g$ is a differentiable function of ${y}^{′}.$\n\nTo solve the Clairaut differential equation\n\n$$\ny=x{y}^{′}+{y}^{′}-3,\n$$\n\nwe start by setting $p={y}^{′}.$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation gives\n\n$$\ny=xp+p-3.\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =(xp+p-3)^{′} \\\\ p & =p+x{p}^{′}+{p}^{′} \\\\ 0 & ={p}^{′}(x+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe product on the right-hand side equals zero, so at least one factor must be zero.\n\nThe singular solution of the Clairaut differential equation is obtained if $x+1=0.$ Then,\n\n$$\nx=-1.\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute this into $y=xp+p-3:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =xp+p-3 \\\\  & =(-1)p+p-3 \\\\  & =-p+p-3 \\\\  & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the explicit form of the singular solution is\n\n$$\ny=-3.\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186332146 17592186332147 17592186332148 17592186332149 17592186332150] "immutable_fields" [[17592186332145 :answer-field/id] [17592186332145 :answer-field/key] [17592186332145 :answer-field/type] [17592186332145 :answer-field/correct] [17592186332145 :answer-field/choices]]}
